# Databricks tier 2's client side (the `spark_connect` compute plug), for
# episode images built with `--warehouse-clients spark` (Dockerfile
# ALKERA_WAREHOUSE_CLIENTS): Apache Spark's own Spark Connect thin client, which
# `SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()` points at $SPARK_REMOTE - the audited
# gRPC proxy native-services hosts in front of the Spark engine
# (gateway/docker/spark/README.md). Pure Python over grpcio: no JVM in the
# agent's image. Apache-2.0 (its PKG-INFO). Not databricks-connect, which is
# under the Databricks License and only connects to the Databricks Services.
#
# The SAME client version as the engine (gateway/docker/spark/fetch.py
# SPARK_VERSION): Spark Connect speaks one protocol per release.
#
# The proxy reads the client's own protobuf definitions
# (pyspark.sql.connect.proto) to audit each plan, so native-services, which runs
# on the episode image, needs this lock as much as the agent does.
#
# grpcio, grpcio-status and googleapis-common-protos are held to the BigQuery
# client lock's pins: an image built with two client sets installs both locks
# into one interpreter, and every package two client locks share must be one
# version (test_toolbelt_roles.test_client_locks_agree_with_each_other).
#
# Compiled here, NOT in pyproject/uv.lock, like every backend's clients
# (docker/lock_warehouse_clients.py): constrained to the base, native and ds
# locks. Regenerate with the command in requirements-spark.lock's header.
pyspark-client==4.2.0
grpcio==1.84.0
grpcio-status==1.84.0
googleapis-common-protos==1.75.5
